Here's how it works.

resisted = rn2(100 + alev - dlev) < mtmp->data->mr

rn2(x) generates a number between 0 and (x - 1).

alev is the attack's level.
dlev is the defender's level, capped at 50.

So, if that random number is less than the creature's MR, the creature
resists the effect.

This means that Rodney, out of the box, always passes his saving throw,
because 'alev' can't be greater than 30 and so a number between 0 and
(99 + alev - dlev) will always be less than 100. This makes him take
half damage from certain effects and renders him immune to certain
others; it is impossible to cancel Rodney (unless you level-drain him
below your own level then cast the cancellation spell on him), and he is
immune to sleep rays.

An arch-lich is immune to wands of cancellation because a random number
between 0 and (99 + 12 - 25) is always less than 86. (The cancellation
spell can sometimes affect arch-liches, if cast by a sufficiently potent
character.)

Death rays don't grant a saving throw. Instead, they kill stone dead any
target they hit who doesn't have one of the perfect boolean defences
against death rays (which include "not being properly alive anyway").
